version="4.4"
function usage {
echo "Author      : Sulfuror, Copyright (c) 2018 <sulfuror@gmail.com>
URL         : https://gitlab.com/sulfuror/rescript.sh
License     : BSD 2-Clause License
Script Name : rescript
Version     : $version
Description : rescript is a bash script created to manage
              backups made with restic program.

'restic is a backup program which allows saving multiple revisions 
of files and directories in an encrypted repository 
stored on different backends.'

For more information about restic visit https://restic.net

This script will run backup, snapshots, forget, prune, check and
stats commands automatically by just indicating the name given
to your repository. e.g.:

  rescript [repo_name]

All restic commands are available within this script except for
[backup], which exists but it will use your configuration
file to perform a backup.

Usage:
  rescript [config_command]
OR
  rescript [repo_name] [rescript_command]
OR
  rescript [repo_name] [restic_command]
  
Rescript configuration commands (these commands does not require
a [repo_name]):
  config                Rescript configuration; set your default
                        text editor, open, delete or modify your
                        configuration and exclusion files.
  editor                Change the default rescript text editor used
                        to open your configuration and exclusions files.
  help                  Display rescript usage.
  install               Install rescript (this just move the script
                        to your .local/bin directory for convenience.
  update                Update is used to update the script itself
                        when a new version is available.
  version               Display rescript version.
  
Rescript commands:
  archive               This command check for differences between
                        the two most recent snapshots, restore deleted
                        files from the latest snapshot and creates a new
                        snapshot with all deleted files using [archive] tag.
  backup                Take a snapshot using the values set in
                        your configuration file.
  changes               This command will automatically select the
                        two most recent snapshots and compare them
                        (it uses 'diff' to compare snapshots).
  checkout              This command will execute check using
                        [--read-data-subset] and selecting a random
                        number between 1-10 out of 10 groups.
  cleanup               Forget and prune according to the policies
                        in your configuration file.
  env                   Display variable values in your configuration
                        file.
  logs                  This command needs a flag to view, list
                        or delete your log files.
  mounter               Mount a restic repo.
  restorer              Restore a restic snapshot.
  snaps                 List all snapshots in the repository. Unlike
                        [snapshots] command, by default this command
                        list all snapshots in compact mode and also
                        has a flag to order snapshots by host or tags.
  unlocker              Remove lock created by rescript.

Rescript glogal flags:
  -h, --help            This flag is available for all rescript commands
                        and it can be used to display the usage for every
                        rescript command.
  -l, --log             This flag is available for all rescript and restic
                        commands. This will create a log file
                        with the command output. You can combine this
                        flag with [--time].
  -t, --time            This flag will display an output with the date,
                        time and duration of the command. You can
                        combine this flag with [--log].

Global flags must be used BEFORE the command. To see usage and
flags available for a specific command type:
  rescript help [command]
"
}
